WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:05.490
Hi there, Steve Kaufman here, and today
I wanna talk about getting started

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:10.620
and some of the common proverbs or
expressions there are around getting

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:14.730
started and how I think we should
get started in learning a language.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:22.500
There is an expression that Aristotle
quotes or quoted, uh, referring to a

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:29.895
proverb that already existed in ancient
Greece, some 300 odd years uh, BCE and

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:32.775
that is well begun is the job half done.

00:00:32.985 --> 00:00:37.125
So it's important to begin the job
well, and then you'll do the job well.

00:00:37.605 --> 00:00:41.025
There's another expression that comes
from about the same period or a little

00:00:41.025 --> 00:00:47.925
earlier attributed to Laozi in, in,
uh, Chinese classics, which says the

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:52.995
thousand li, mile call it the thousand
mile march begins with the first step.

00:00:53.625 --> 00:00:57.945
So two different proverb talking about
the importance of getting started.

00:00:58.575 --> 00:01:02.775
Uh, I thought about this because
last little while I've kind of not

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:06.735
been doing much language learning,
haven't done much with my Farsi, uh,

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:11.145
so I don't listen to content, then
I don't speak to my Iranian tutor.

00:01:11.595 --> 00:01:15.914
And uh, that kind of was the
situation for quite a few days.

00:01:16.545 --> 00:01:18.045
And then I had to clean up the kitchen.

00:01:18.045 --> 00:01:19.575
There was quite a big mess there.

00:01:19.725 --> 00:01:24.705
So I'll put on my Iranian history
series and the thing is, once you

00:01:24.705 --> 00:01:28.455
get started again, all of a sudden
everything comes back to life.

00:01:28.455 --> 00:01:32.685
So now I'm interested again in
learning Persian, and then I say,

00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:36.405
yeah, I'm gonna book another session
with Sahra online, with my tutor.

00:01:36.735 --> 00:01:40.035
So sometimes, you know, you can
hang back and the same is true of

00:01:40.039 --> 00:01:44.295
exercising, hang back and not do
something for something for a while.

00:01:44.685 --> 00:01:46.365
Convince yourself you don't
wanna do it or whatever.

00:01:46.885 --> 00:01:49.845
Once you get started
you're back into it again.

00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:52.545
So it's very important
to take that first step.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:58.755
As Laozi said, the first step on that
thousand mile thousand li march gets

00:01:58.755 --> 00:02:02.055
you going, and once you get going,
chances are you'll stay with it.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:03.495
Hopefully get some good habits.

00:02:03.945 --> 00:02:09.195
Also, getting back to Aristotle's,
uh, the proverb that he quoted well

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:13.695
begun is the job have done, and his
inference was that if you start something

00:02:14.255 --> 00:02:15.856
properly, you're likely to complete it.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.700
So assuming we want to get into language
learning or we wanna get back to the

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:25.920
language who we were learning, so then
what is properly, what is well done?

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:30.690
Well, in terms of a new language,
I'm convinced that our mini

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:33.660
stories at LingQ, you won't be
surprised to hear me say that, to

00:02:33.665 --> 00:02:35.370
me that's the place to get started.

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:40.695
You shouldn't get started on lists of
colors or lists, you know, um, members

00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:45.585
of the family or any, or people, uh,
you know, at the train station or any

00:02:45.585 --> 00:02:49.995
of the traditional stuff that's put
in language, uh, books, you shouldn't

00:02:50.265 --> 00:02:52.875
try upfront to understand the grammar.

00:02:52.875 --> 00:02:55.995
You can maybe have an overview,
which is easy enough to do.

00:02:55.995 --> 00:03:01.155
I mean, you can just Google or go to
chat GPT and ask for, you know, a summary

00:03:01.155 --> 00:03:05.805
of the basics of Punjabi grammar and
you'll get it, but you won't remember it.

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:08.355
But it's kind of a bit of an
overview, which is fine to have.

00:03:08.985 --> 00:03:13.155
Which is fine to have, but really
to get started, well begun, you

00:03:13.155 --> 00:03:14.925
have to get into the language.

00:03:14.955 --> 00:03:19.305
You have to get into content
which has a lot of repetition and

00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:21.825
hopefully high frequency verbs.

00:03:21.975 --> 00:03:25.485
As I've said before, the reason
I think our mini stories are so

00:03:25.485 --> 00:03:30.285
superior to most beginner content
is that there's so much repetition.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:35.340
Uh, the same high frequency verbs
repeat within each story, and

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.410
they continue showing up in other
stories over the full 60 stories.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:44.250
Whereas typically in a beginner
book, you're moving from the train

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:49.770
station to going through customs to
visiting the doctor, uh, post office,

00:03:50.295 --> 00:03:53.055
and the vocabulary doesn't repeat.

00:03:53.055 --> 00:03:56.265
It doesn't repeat within each
story or within each lesson.

00:03:56.265 --> 00:03:58.425
It doesn't repeat across the lessons.

00:03:58.484 --> 00:04:03.225
And we need so much repetition initially
to get a sense of the language that I

00:04:03.225 --> 00:04:05.445
think that that is the place to start.

00:04:05.715 --> 00:04:07.445
Not super boring stu...

00:04:07.460 --> 00:04:08.815
hello, my name is...

00:04:09.405 --> 00:04:10.245
not necessary.

00:04:10.245 --> 00:04:11.144
It'll show up.

00:04:11.505 --> 00:04:16.410
Uh, there's no need to, you know, at
the very beginning, how old are you?

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:17.490
It'll come up.

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:22.950
And how often in conversation or
in listening to the language do you

00:04:23.430 --> 00:04:25.740
actually need to say how old you are?

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.280
Un unless you are deliberately
creating a conversation for the

00:04:29.285 --> 00:04:32.550
sake of having a conversation,
then that's likely to come up.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.190
But in reality, it doesn't
come up that often.

00:04:35.370 --> 00:04:39.330
And if it does come up, if you have a
good base in the language, you'll quickly

00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.580
learn how to say, how old are you?

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:45.705
Or, you know, whatever, however
many years old, it's far better

00:04:45.705 --> 00:04:48.605
to sort of jump in midstream.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:53.580
Midstream, albeit shallow water,
like the mini stories with a lot

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.800
of repetition, but it is midstream.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.250
It's into the language.

00:04:57.255 --> 00:04:59.100
You're starting to absorb the language.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:00.540
You're listening to the language.

00:05:00.719 --> 00:05:01.890
You're reading the language.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:03.270
You're looking up words, you're listening.

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:08.440
Again, that's how you get into
learning a language and if you

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:12.580
stay with it for a few months, you
actually get a sense of the language.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.810
And with that sense of the language
language, you can now start to make sense

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:19.450
of some of the grammatical explanations.

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:25.210
Uh, don't try to, you know, study
conjugation tables, declension tables,

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:30.370
remember rules, all of that is gonna
set you back, discourage you, make you

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:31.870
think the language is very difficult.

00:05:32.110 --> 00:05:35.895
Just get into the language.

00:05:36.015 --> 00:05:38.745
You know, I can mix my metaphors
here, you know, jump right in,

00:05:38.745 --> 00:05:40.365
but then I said shallow water.

00:05:40.575 --> 00:05:45.135
So you don't wanna jump into shallow
water, but get going into sort of

00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:50.175
the mainstream of the language,
albeit with relatively simple, high

00:05:50.175 --> 00:05:54.675
frequency vocabulary, and you'll get
a head start on learning the language.

00:05:55.125 --> 00:06:00.975
The language will be well begun,
and therefore, eventually will

00:06:00.975 --> 00:06:02.595
be hopefully a job well done.

00:06:02.945 --> 00:06:06.870
So just thought I would share with you
that sense of the importance of getting

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.630
started and getting started the right way.

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:11.010
Thanks for listening.

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:11.890
Bye for now.

